  <qti-item-body>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_TEACH" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <h4>Word Choice</h4>
        <p><em>formal / literary</em></p>
        <p>Archaic is a scholarly word for things that belong to a vanished past. In everyday speech, say 'old-fashioned' or 'outdated.' Reach for archaic when describing vocabulary, customs, or styles that clearly belong to a historical era.</p>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_KEEP" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <h4>Putting it all together</h4>
        <p><em>Read the passage, then answer Parts A, B and C.</em></p>
        <p>Tracing her family's farm across an 1843 county map, Elena kept stumbling over villages she could not find in any modern atlas.</p>
        <p>The settlements still existed; only their names had moved on.</p>
        <p>The map's labels were archaic, recording what each place was called before the railway companies renamed whole districts to suit their timetables.</p>
        <p>Locals had not spoken the old names in four generations, and the road signs Elena passed that afternoon bore no trace of them.</p>
        <p>Only in the parish registers, and on maps like hers, did the older names survive at all.</p>
      </qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_Q1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="R1" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
          <qti-prompt>In the passage, archaic most precisely means that the map's labels are…</qti-prompt>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">extremely old and therefore of great historical value</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">once in common use, later given up entirely</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">no longer official, though still common in local speech</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">recorded incorrectly by the mapmakers of the day</qti-simple-choice>
        </qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1A" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>The passage never appraises the map or trades on its age; Elena's problem is not how old the paper is. Precision is the test here, and this choice measures the wrong dimension.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1C" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Half of this claim is checkable against the passage itself: it reports plainly whether these place-labels survived in anyone's everyday talk. Test the 'local speech' half against that report before accepting this option.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1D" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Nothing in the passage faults the mapmakers. Elena's trouble comes from what changed after the map was printed, not from errors on it.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q1correct" outcome-identifier="FB1" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Right. The passage pairs two facts: these labels once served as the everyday way of referring to each place, and that everyday use later ended completely. Both halves matter, and only this choice carries both.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_Q2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="R2" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
          <qti-prompt>Which detail from the passage best supports your answer to Part A?</qti-prompt>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">recording what each place was called before the railway companies renamed whole districts</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">The settlements still existed</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">Locals had not spoken the old names in four generations</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">villages she could not find in any modern atlas</qti-simple-choice>
        </qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2A" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>An official renaming tells you what the railway companies decided, not what the people living there went on saying afterward. The decisive question is still open.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2B" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>This clears away one wrong explanation — the villages themselves never disappeared — but it says nothing about the names.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2D" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Elena's search trouble could have many causes: the map's scale, shifted spellings, an atlas that skips small places. It reports her difficulty, not the fate of the names.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q2correct" outcome-identifier="FB2" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Right. This is the passage's one direct report of what ordinary speakers did with these names over time, and it is the detail Part A's answer stands on.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="on" outcome-identifier="C_Q3" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body>
        <p>In which of these sentences is archaic used correctly?</p>
        <qti-choice-interaction response-identifier="R3" shuffle="true" max-choices="1">
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="A">Her archaic outfit looked very modern and fashionable.</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="B">The recipe was too archaic even though it's made daily.</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="C">That archaic dance is performed at every school event.</qti-simple-choice>
          <qti-simple-choice identifier="D">The historian discovered archaic texts from the fifth century.</qti-simple-choice>
        </qti-choice-interaction></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q3A" outcome-identifier="FB3" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>This contradicts itself: archaic means belonging to an earlier era and no longer in use, so an archaic outfit can't look 'very modern and fashionable' — modern is the word's direct opposite.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q3B" outcome-identifier="FB3" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>A recipe that's made daily is still in current use. Archaic means old and no longer used — the 'even though it's made daily' clause is exactly what rules the word out.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q3C" outcome-identifier="FB3" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>A dance performed at every school event is still in active use today. Archaic specifically means out of use — not merely old — so it doesn't fit something performed all the time.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
    <qti-feedback-block identifier="q3D" outcome-identifier="FB3" show-hide="show">
      <qti-content-body><p>Correct. Texts from the fifth century belong to an earlier period, written in language that has long since fallen out of use — old and no longer current is exactly what archaic means.</p></qti-content-body>
    </qti-feedback-block>
  </qti-item-body>

